  "prediction": "The report highlights the demographic trends in the technology workforce and the efforts of the U.S. Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC) and the Department of Labor's Office of Federal Contract Compliance Programs (OFCCP) to oversee technology companies and contractors' compliance with equal employment opportunity and affirmative action requirements.\n\nThe technology sector has major employment hubs across the country, including the San Francisco Bay area, the greater New York City region, and the Washington-Arlington-Alexandria region. However, female, Black, and Hispanic workers comprise a smaller proportion of technology workers compared to their representation in the general workforce from 2005 through 2015. These groups are also less represented among technology workers inside the technology sector than outside it.\n\nThe EEOC and OFCCP have missions to combat discrimination and support equal employment opportunity for U.S. workers. However, weaknesses in their processes impact the effectiveness of their efforts. The EEOC has not been consistently capturing information on industry codes, which impedes its ability to conduct industry sector analysis. The OFCCP faces delays in its compliance review process and has not analyzed its closed evaluations to understand the causes of these delays.\n\nThe report makes six recommendations to improve oversight of federal contractors, including requiring contractors to disaggregate demographic data for the purpose of setting placement goals in their affirmative action programs. The Department of Labor agreed with four of the five recommendations, while the EEOC did not agree or disagree with the recommendation to develop a timeline to complete the planned effort to clean IMS data for a one-year period.\n\nThe report also highlights the importance of addressing the underrepresentation of female, Black, and Hispanic workers in the technology sector. The EEOC and OFCCP have taken steps to address this issue, including research and outreach efforts. However, more needs to be done to ensure that technology companies and contractors are complying with equal employment opportunity and affirmative action requirements.\n\nOverall, the report emphasizes the need for the EEOC and OFCCP to improve their processes and address the underrepresentation of underrepresented groups in the technology sector. This includes requiring contractors to disaggregate demographic data, analyzing industry sector data, and addressing delays in compliance reviews.",
